Q: How can I get the size of input from the console using C++? A: <p>It's funny to me how many people suggest <code>std::string</code> (that is sort of unlimited buffer) without actually attempting any kind of implementation without fixed buffer. Well for <code>unix</code> type system you may want to look at <a href="https://stackoverflow.com/questions/4411852/proper-efficient-way-to-determine-size-of-stdin-in-c">stat implementation</a> but for any other, there are few issues that need to be resolved:</p>
<ol>
<li><p>Memory management : Your computer has fixed size of memory it can use. Due to me writing this in 2025 and forward, its about <a href="https://askubuntu.com/questions/299058/is-there-a-default-limit-to-the-amount-of-memory-a-process-may-use">2-4 KB</a> (but that also can be increased in future)</p>
</li>
<li><p>The true requirement for longer text formats is actually done in parts. Without increasing memory limit per program you'd have to share that wall of text you'll be getting across hard disk and program memory. This is much much slower than what you are used to, and while it does have its benefits and uses - they are extremely rare.</p>
</li>
<li><p>This is also memory taxing and complex since it would require memory swapping and juggling while also breaking environment. So edge cases should be arbitrarily set in order to gain a bit of safety. Think about it like this : every time we exceed allocated memory buffer, we will have to move it and reinitialize it with larger size while also freeing memory that it owned and doing substitution.</p>
</li>
<li><p>This can be potentially security flawed, thus why its generally avoided.</p>
</li>
</ol>
<p>But with all that, here we go! Firstly we will need to define fixed buffer we will use as memory block, sort of unit of measurement to ease up copying in order to copy in bulk not char by char.</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>const unsigned short BLOCK_SIZE = 4096u; // memory block, minimum 4 kb
/*
    theoretically we can store maximum of data as typed integer can allow us
    for c++ is unsigned long long int - 18.4467 Exabytes 
    for anything greater one dives into building your own types
    which isn't part of this question.
*/

struct temp_buffer
{
    char *block = nullptr;
    unsigned long long int mem_size = 0; 

    temp_buffer(unsigned long long int mem = BLOCK_SIZE)
    :block(nullptr)
    ,mem_size(mem)
    {
        // heap allocation of block
        this-&gt;block = new char[this-&gt;mem_size]{0};
    }

    ~temp_buffer()
    {
        if ( this-&gt;block != nullptr)
        {
                // deleting entire heap allocated block
                delete[] this-&gt;block;
        }
    }

    //quck validity checks
    operator bool() const
    {
        return (this-&gt;block != nullptr);
    }

    bool operator!() const
    {
        return (this-&gt;block == nullptr);
    }

    // quick rule of 5
    temp_buffer( const temp_buffer &amp;tb)
    :block(nullptr)
    ,mem_size(tb.mem_size)
    {
        // heap allocation of block
        this-&gt;block = new char[this-&gt;mem_size]{0};
        for (  unsigned long long int i = 0; i &lt; this-&gt;mem_size; i++)
        {
            // copying of the block
            this-&gt;block[i] = tb.block[i];
        }
    }

    temp_buffer &amp;operator=( const temp_buffer &amp;tb)
    {
        if ( this-&gt;block != nullptr)
        {
            delete[] this-&gt;block;
        }
        // heap allocation of block
        this-&gt;block = new char[this-&gt;mem_size]{0};
        for (  unsigned long long int i = 0; i &lt; this-&gt;mem_size; i++)
        {
            // copying of the block
            this-&gt;block[i] = tb.block[i];
        }
        return *this;
    }

    temp_buffer ( temp_buffer &amp;&amp; tb)
    :block(tb.block)
    ,mem_size(tb.mem_size)
    {
        tb.block = nullptr;
        tb.mem_size = 0;
    }

    temp_buffer &amp;operator=( temp_buffer &amp;&amp; tb)
    {
        if ( this-&gt;block != nullptr)
        {
            delete[] this-&gt;block;
        }

        this-&gt;block = tb.block;
        this-&gt;mem_size = tb.mem_size;
        tb.block = nullptr;
        tb.mem_size = 0;
        return *this;
    }
};
</code></pre>
<p>So now we can copy it, move it, initialize it to a different size while also handling memory management. This will help us abstract whole functionality a bit more into few steps:</p>
<pre><code>std::istream &amp;operator&gt;&gt;(std::istream &amp;is, temp_buffer &amp;tb)
{
    // if temp buffer is already initialised

    temp_buffer temp; // initialises to 4096 b
    char *tb_loc = temp.block; // starting position of block in tb   
    char data = 0;

    while ( is &gt;&gt; data)
    {
        // if temp.mem_size is reached:
            // build new_temp 'temp_buffer' with block expansion (4096 Bytes)
            // substitute temp with new_temp
            // update location position of tb_loc
        // feed gotten data into temp buffer via tb_loc
    }    

    // move temp to tb    

    return is;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Where with this we are basically handling slowly building <code>tb</code> in 4096 increments till we reach <code>end of file</code>  for <code>std::istream</code> or any error arises. These errors should happen! Computer needs to be able to &quot;take care&quot; of itself, so if any error happens we should respect it.</p>
<p>However with these abstractions its better to go &quot;butt first&quot; and solve easiest parts primarely.</p>
<pre><code>std::istream &amp;operator&gt;&gt;(std::istream &amp;is, temp_buffer &amp;tb)
{
    // if temp buffer is already initialised - destroy it
    if ( tb )
    {
        delete[] tb.block;
        tb.mem_size = 0;
    }

    // byte tracker for read memory
    unsigned long long int count = 0;

    temp_buffer temp; // initialises to 4096 b
    char *tb_loc = temp.block; // starting position of block in tb   
    char data = 0;

    while ( is &gt;&gt; data)
    {
        // if temp.mem_size is reached:

            // build new_temp 'temp_buffer' with block expansion (4096 Bytes)
            // substitute temp with new_temp
            // update location position of tb_loc

        // feed gotten data into temp buffer via tb_loc
    }    

    // move temp to tb
    tb.mem_size = count;
    tb.block = new char[tb.mem_size]{0};
    for( unsigned long long int i =0; i &lt; tb.mem_size; i++)
    {
        tb.block[i] = temp.block[i];
    }

    return is;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here any <code>temp_buffer</code> that needs to be read into will automatically be assumed that to be too small, so we will delete it and reinitialise it to 0. That way we don't have to tacke memory size right from start, but can focus on swapping and exchanging without more complications.</p>
<p>Secondly, we would want to copy only existing block of data even if our temp will grow. That way we get only what was in <code>std::istream</code> and nothing else. Due to how we formulated pseudo check if memory size is reached, it will start new loop after we have incremented the value of counter, so counter will always be updated to next character we need to fill.</p>
<p>Then we can go and handle how buffer will be filled:</p>
<pre><code>std::istream &amp;operator&gt;&gt;(std::istream &amp;is, temp_buffer &amp;tb)
{
    // if temp buffer is already initialized - destroy it
    if ( tb )
    {
        delete[] tb.block;
        tb.mem_size = 0;
    }

    // byte tracker for read memory
    unsigned long long int count = 0;

    temp_buffer temp; // initialises to 4096 b
    char *tb_loc = temp.block; // starting position of block in tb   
    char data = 0;

    while ( is &gt;&gt; data)
    {
        // if temp.mem_size is reached:

            // build new_temp 'temp_buffer' with block expansion (4096 Bytes)
            // substitute temp with new_temp
            // update location position of tb_loc

        // feed gotten data into temp buffer via tb_loc
        (*tb_loc) = data; // assing data to current pointer value 
        tb_loc++;
        counter++;        // increment counter for one more.
    }    

    // move temp to tb
    tb.mem_size = count;
    tb.block = new char[tb.mem_size]{0};
    for( unsigned long long int i =0; i &lt; tb.mem_size; i++)
    {
        tb.block[i] = temp.block[i];
    }

    return is;
}
</code></pre>
<p>With next lines we update current position of pointer to next memory byte, and update counter to that next byte as well. So we can feed chars for as long as there is data to be saved. This helps us handle memory management better, since counter is always +1 than location in memory we save, we can simply check via:</p>
<pre><code>std::istream &amp;operator&gt;&gt;(std::istream &amp;is, temp_buffer &amp;tb)
{
    // if temp buffer is already initialised - destroy it
    if ( tb )
    {
        delete[] tb.block;
        tb.mem_size = 0;
    }

    // byte tracker for read memory
    unsigned long long int count = 0;

    temp_buffer temp; // initialises to 4096 b
    char *tb_loc = temp.block; // starting position of block in tb   
    char data = 0;

    while ( is &gt;&gt; data)
    {
        // if temp.mem_size is reached:

            // build new_temp 'temp_buffer' with block expansion (4096 Bytes)
            // substitute temp with new_temp
            // update location position of tb_loc

        // feed gotten data into temp buffer via tb_loc
    }    

    // move temp to tb
    tb.mem_size = count;
    tb.block = new char[tb.mem_size]{0};
    for( unsigned long long int i =0; i &lt; tb.mem_size; i++)
    {
        tb.block[i] = temp.block[i];
    }

    return is;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Here any <code>temp_buffer</code> that needs to be read into will automatically be assumed that to be too small, so we will delete it and reinitialise it to 0. That way we don't have to tacke memory size right from start, but can focus on swapping and exchanging without more complications.</p>
<p>Secondly, we would want to copy only existing block of data even if our temp will grow. That way we get only what was in <code>std::istream</code> and nothing else. Due to how we formulated pseudo check if memory size is reached, it will start new loop after we have incremented the value of counter, so counter will always be updated to next character we need to fill.</p>
<p>Then we can go and handle how buffer will be filled:</p>
<pre><code>std::istream &amp;operator&gt;&gt;(std::istream &amp;is, temp_buffer &amp;tb)
{
    // if temp buffer is already initialized - destroy it
    if ( tb )
    {
        delete[] tb.block;
        tb.mem_size = 0;
    }

    // byte tracker for read memory
    unsigned long long int count = 0;

    temp_buffer temp; // initializes to 4096 b
    char *tb_loc = temp.block; // starting position of block in tb   
    char data = 0;

    while ( is &gt;&gt; data)
    {
        // if temp.mem_size is reached:
        if ( count == temp.mem_size)
        {
            // build new_temp 'temp_buffer' with block expansion (4096 Bytes)
            temp_buffer new_temp(temp.mem_size + BLOCK_SIZE);
            // substitute temp with new_temp
            temp = std::move(new_temp); // deletes old one
            // update location position of tb_loc
            tb_loc = temp.block + count - 1;
        }
        // feed gotten data into temp buffer via tb_loc
        (*tb_loc) = data; // assigning data to current pointer value 
        tb_loc++;
        count++;        // increment counter for one more.
    }    

    // move temp to tb
    tb.mem_size = count;
    tb.block = new char[tb.mem_size]{0};
    for( unsigned long long int i =0; i &lt; tb.mem_size; i++)
    {
        tb.block[i] = temp.block[i];
    }

    return is;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Is this smart thing to do? Nope, is it sometimes a requirement? Yes. This can be used to allow for variable sized memory probing for external programs. Lets say you make some virtual computer architecture and need to dump entire virutal RAM to file after each operation to debug some execution bug. Youd probably want this kind of logging so it can be parsed by some other text based app.</p>
<p>Outside of that, you shouldn't be accepting any input from <code>std::cin</code> that you don't know or which format you don't support. This is another whole can of worms, but in short by overriding <code>std::istream &amp;operator&gt;&gt;(std::istream &amp;, some object &amp;);</code> you are establishing format you wish, so only thing that matters are delimiters. Then all you need to do is build some sort of data structure to put all that data into and <code>while(is &gt;&gt; myobj)</code> works as well.</p>
<p>Disclaimer : <code>DO NOT COMPILE THIS CODE</code> its effectively <strong>endless while loop</strong>.</p>
<p>Normally youd put edge case to terminate reading from <code>std::cin</code> depending on what you need. One might want to terminate on 'STOP' on specific string or command character. This is also very unsafe due to use of control characters that your system can execute without your knowing. So basically, this is how its done in most general terms, if you need this for your code - you better know what you are doing.</p>
